      "filename": "Canada de las Uvas, or Tejon Pass in Sierra Nevada, California, 1,690 miles west of Missouri River. (Boston Public Library).jpg",
      "title": "File:Canada de las Uvas, or Tejon Pass in Sierra Nevada, California, 1,690 miles west of Missouri River. (Boston Public Library).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/1/1a/Canada_de_las_Uvas%2C_or_Tejon_Pass_in_Sierra_Nevada%2C_California%2C_1%2C690_miles_west_of_Missouri_River._%28Boston_Public_Library%29.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canada_de_las_Uvas,_or_Tejon_Pass_in_Sierra_Nevada,_California,_1,690_miles_west_of_Missouri_River._(Boston_Public_Library).jpg",
      "width": 1500,
      "height": 1004,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Gardner, Alexander, 1821-1882",
      "description": "File name: 10_08_000106\n\nTitle: Canada de las Uvas, or Tejon Pass in Sierra Nevada, California, 1,690 miles west of Missouri River.\n\nCreator/Contributor: Gardner, Alexander, 1821-1882 (photographer)\n\nDate issued: 1869 (approximate)\n\nDate created: 1868 (approximate)\n\nPhysical description: 1 photographic print : albumen ; image 15 x 20.4 cm.\n\nGenre: Albumen prints; Landscape photographs; Portrait photographs \n\nSubjects:  Tejon Pass, California; Tehachapi Mountains; Sierra Nevada; Passes (Landforms); Canyons;\n\nNotes: On March 9, 1869 a joint resolution of Congress authorized the changing of the Union Pacific Railway Company, Eastern Division's name to the Kansas Pacific Railway Company.;  Although all of the photographs published in Across the Continent on the Kansas Pacific Railroad (Route of the 35th Parallel) are attributed to Gardner, some of the images may have been taken by one of the other photographers Gardner supervised on the expedition. \"As official photographer for the expedition, Gardner was allowed to published all the expedition photographs under his name.  In 1867 he stated in a deposition that although a photograph was identified on the mount as a 'Photograph by A. Gardner,' it simply meant that it was printed or copied in his gallery; he was not necessarily the photographer.  \nThe other photographers on the expedition were Dr. William A. Bell, William R. Pywell, and Gardner's son, Lawrence, who apprenticed on the expedition.\" [Katz, D. Mark (1991). Witness to an era: the life and photographs of Alexander Gardner: the Civil War, Lincoln, and the West. Nashville, Tennessee: Rutledge Hill. Page 220];\nImages most likely published in 1869. \"Along with images made by photographers under his [Alexander Gardner's] supervision, his photographs were published in an album titled Across the Continent on the Kansas Pacific Railyway (Route of the 35th Parallel), offered for sale on April, 1869.\" [Marien, Mary Warner (2006). Photography: a cultural history. London: Laurence King Publishing Ltd. Page 132]\n\nItem/Page/Plate: No. 106\n\nSource: Across the continent on the Kansas Pacific Railroad: route of the 35th parallel / Alex. Gardner, photographer.\n\nLocation: Boston Public Library, Print Department",
      "dateCreated": "1869<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P571,+1869-00-00T00:00:00Z/9</div>",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "san-joaquin-valley-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "9qd8",
      "smallPath": "_m/9/q/d/8/san-joaquin-valley-wp/canada-de-las-uvas-or-tejon-pa-small.webp"
    },
    {
      "filename": "\"San Joaquin Valley, California - Contract Labor\" - NARA - 532157.tif",
      "title": "File:\"San Joaquin Valley, California - Contract Labor\" - NARA - 532157.tif",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/97/%22San_Joaquin_Valley%2C_California_-_Contract_Labor%22_-_NARA_-_532157.tif?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%22San_Joaquin_Valley,_California_-_Contract_Labor%22_-_NARA_-_532157.tif",
      "width": 3000,
      "height": 2998,
      "mime": "image/tiff",
      "license": "Public domain",
      "artist": "Rondal Partridge",
      "description": "Scope and content:  \"San Joaquin Valley, California. Contract Labor. This scene is near the camp of a labor contractor. The labor of the young agricultural worker is contracted, but their spare time is wasted. Living in the contractor's camp paying the contractor for his tent and camping facilities in deductions from their pay, the young migratory agricultural worker becomes a commodity which the contrcator sells to the highest bidder among the small farmers anxxious to produce extra labor with which to harvest their crops. These pictures were taken in the vicinity of a Copus Camp, which is a labor contractor operating in a number of California crop centers and in Idaho, Utah, and Colorado. Only those workers who have been issued \"Copus Crew\" buttons identifying themselves as \"paying guests\" of the Copus Camp (they rent tents and ground space) are entitled to employment in a Copus contracted field. But the possession of a button is not assurance of immediate work. Arriving at the scene of work promised by the contracto.....\"",
      "dateCreated": "9 April 1940<div style=\"display: none;\">date QS:P571,+1940-04-09T00:00:00Z/11</div>",
      "sourceType": "commons-category",
      "sourceArticleId": "san-joaquin-valley-wp",
      "sourceGeohash4": "9qd8",
      "mediumPath": "_m/9/q/d/8/san-joaquin-valley-wp/san-joaquin-valley-california--medium.webp",
      "smallPath": "_m/9/q/d/8/san-joaquin-valley-wp/san-joaquin-valley-california--small.webp"
    },
